Q: when do you put a network load balencer in front of an application load balencer
A: 
1. Static IP addresses: When you need fixed, static IP addresses for your application. NLBs provide static IP addresses (one per Availability Zone), while ALBs use dynamic IP addresses. This is useful for allowlisting with external partners who require fixed IPs.
2. TLS termination at different layers: When you want to perform TLS termination at the network layer (NLB) but still need application layer (L7) routing capabilities of the ALB.
3. Cross-region load balancing: When implementing a global load balancing strategy that requires both network-level routing and application-aware routing.
4. DDoS protection: When you want to add an extra layer of protection. The NLB can absorb some types of network-level DDoS attacks while the ALB handles application-specific traffic.
5. Preserving client IP addresses: When you need to preserve the original client IP address through to your applications. NLBs preserve the client's IP address, which can then be forwarded to the ALB.
6. Private Link integration: When using AWS Private Link services, which require NLB, but you still want the routing capabilities of an ALB.
7. Protocol support: When you need to support both TCP/UDP protocols (supported by NLB) and HTTP/HTTPS with content-based routing (supported by ALB).



