Q: What's the difference between an Application Load Balancer (ALB) and a Network Load Balancer (NLB)?
A:
Application Load Balancer (ALB):
* Layer 7 (Application layer)
* Handles HTTP/HTTPS traffic
* Can route based on content (URL path, host headers)
* Best for web applications
* Slower than NLB but more features
Network Load Balancer (NLB):
* Layer 4 (Transport layer)
* Handles TCP/UDP/TLS traffic
* Routes based on IP protocol data
* Best for extreme performance needs or non-HTTP/S protocols
* Ultra-low latency
* Static IP addresses for your load balancer

Q: What's the difference between Amazon SQS Standard and FIFO queues?
A:
SQS Standard:
* Unlimited throughput
* At-least-once delivery (might deliver same message multiple times)
* Best-effort ordering (as you mentioned, messages can be out of order)
* Usually used for high-throughput applications where order isn't critical
SQS FIFO:
* First-In-First-Out delivery (as you mentioned)
* Exactly-once processing
* Limited to 300 messages per second (3000 with batching)
* Messages processed and delivered in strict order
* Support for message groups
* Usually used when order of operations is important

Q: What's the difference between AWS Direct Connect and VPN connections?
AWS Direct Connect:
* Physical dedicated private connection to AWS
* Consistent network performance (no internet dependencies)
* Higher bandwidth options (1Gbps to 100Gbps)
* More expensive
* Takes weeks/months to set up
